Building zola
=============

This package patches one of its own dependencies during the build. That is
unusual enough to be worth stating outright, and Debian Policy 4.14 asks for it
here rather than only in a rules comment.

What is patched, and why
------------------------

minify-html-common 0.0.3's build script deserialises its HTML data into
HashMaps and then emits generated Rust by iterating them, so `attrs.rs` and
`entities.rs` come out in a different order on every build: Rust's RandomState
reseeds per process. The difference propagates through minify_html into zola's
own `site` crate, and it was the only reason this package did not reproduce
byte-for-byte.

crates.io has published nothing newer than 0.0.3 (2025-10-24), so there is no
version to move to, and the crate is a build-time dependency rather than
anything in the source tree, so a quilt patch cannot reach it.

How
---

`debian/rules` sets CARGO_HOME inside the build tree, so every build extracts
its own copy of the registry. `override_dh_auto_configure` then runs
`cargo fetch`, which populates `registry/src/`, and rewrites the two
declarations to BTreeMap with sed.

All three edits are asserted afterwards. If upstream changes those lines the
build fails loudly, rather than quietly going back to producing a package that
cannot be verified.

Nothing third-party is vendored into this repository. Vendoring the crate was
the first approach and worked; it was rejected because 360 KB of upstream
source would need re-checking on every zola bump.

When to remove it
-----------------

When minify-html-common ships a release whose build script emits in a stable
order. At that point drop `override_dh_auto_configure` and this file, and
confirm two builds still produce the same .deb.

Checking it still works
-----------------------

Two builds of the same source must produce an identical .deb:

    docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/target" ghcr.io/pkghaus/deb-builder:unstable
    sha256sum debs/zola_*.deb

or, against a published record, `verify/rebuild.sh` in pkghaus/apt.
